    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "High athletic identity paired with low level of sport competition was associated with disordered eating. The combination of high athletic identity and high perceived level of competition was not associated with disordered eating."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "The study was cross sectional. Physical activity and sport competition level were self reported. Mobile technology (e.g., fitbit) would provide more precise physical activity data, as well as other related variables, such as heartrate, to investigate if intensity of exercise activity in-and out-of sport has associations with athletic identity and disordered eating behaviors. This study used the AIMS-Plus, which as limited psychometric validity. This study used a collegiate sample and findings may not generalize to other populations."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Longitudinal investigations of athletic identity, sport competition level and disordered eating among broad populations of athletes (e.g., currently competitive, previously competitive, and recreational) at various stages of sport participation (high shcool, collegiate) are needed."
